# claude-router rules: which claude-acs account a `claude` launch uses.
# One rule per line:  <account> [condition ...]   first match wins; every condition must hold.
# A line with no conditions always matches: put it last as a catch-all, or leave it out to
# fall back to the default account (claude-acs df).
#
# Conditions (zsh extended globs; * also matches /; prefix ! to negate):
#   path=<glob>     the launch folder or anything inside it (~ allowed)
#   remote=<glob>   git remote get-url origin (case-insensitive)
#   repo=<glob>     repo name, from origin, else the folder holding .git/.bare (case-insensitive)
#   branch=<glob>   current branch
#   host=<glob>     short hostname (case-insensitive)
#   env:NAME=<glob> an environment variable; env:NAME=?* means "set and non-empty"
#
# These only apply when nobody chose an account already: an explicit `claude-acs sw`,
# a herdr session routed by name, CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR set by a script, or
# CLAUDE_ROUTE_ACCOUNT all win. A repo pin (claude-route pin <acct>) beats these rules.
# Check a folder with: claude-route which [dir]
#
# Examples:
#   personal  remote=*[:/]Alancs92/*                 personal GitHub repos
#   harrison  remote=*[:/]harrison-ai/*              work org repos
#   personal  path=~/repos/side-projects
#   personal  env:ORCA_PANE_KEY=?* repo=textx-v2     only inside Orca, only this repo
#   harrison  branch=V2B-*
